      "text": [
        {
          "id": "title",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "#2 · Hyginus, Fabulae 164; Augustine, City of God 18.9; the Acropolis Museum's pediment record",
          "title": "The flood and its variant aftermaths",
          "italic": "The variants demonstrate the aftermath differing while the retained-contrary relation holds — no mythic action is asserted as established history.",
          "body": "",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.05,
          "width": 560,
          "size": 30,
          "align": "left"
        },
        {
          "id": "body1",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "The verdict does not dissolve Poseidon's force: in anger he floods the Thriasian plain and lays Attica under the sea. Hyginus qualifies the aftermath: Neptune intends to inundate the land but Mercury prevents him on Jupiter's orders, and Minerva founds Athens in her own name — the first city established in the land. Augustine sources a different sequence through Varro: an olive and water appear; Cecrops consults Delphi; the naming goes to the citizens; the women, outnumbering the",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.47,
          "width": 620,
          "size": 14,
          "align": "left",
          "w": 520
        },
        {
          "id": "body2",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "men by one, choose Minerva; Neptune devastates the land; to appease him the women lose their votes, the right to give children maternal names, and the designation Athenian — and Augustine presses the reversal: the goddess whose voters won cannot protect them. The Parthenon's west pediment gives the contest its monumental civic image, its trident-action readable as withdrawal or as striking to flood; monument, reconstructed scene and literary narrative remain different witnesses.\n\nOccurrences: the Thriasian flood (#1, #2); the prevented inundation (#2); the women's vote and its removal (#2); the pediment's double reading (#2).",
          "x": 0.6,
          "y": 0.47,
          "width": 505,
          "size": 14,
          "align": "left"
        }
      ],
